{"p":"/matchmaking","t":"Matchmaking","d":"Match players to servers inside your game.","h":"<p>Matchmaking is the process of matching players to servers based on player characteristics like age, language, skill level, and other gameplay preferences.</p>\n<p><a class=\"yt\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fUMlnldI53A\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Watch this video on YouTube ↗</a></p>\n\n<h2>How matchmaking works</h2>\n<p>When a player tries to join your game, the matchmaking service finds all eligible servers that the player can join, scores these servers using signals, and matches the player to the server with the highest score.</p>\n<p>The matchmaking flow works like this:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A player requests to join a place.</li>\n<li>Matchmaking finds eligible servers that the player can join, filtering out servers that are full, private, reserved, or about to be shut down.</li>\n<li>Matchmaking scores all eligible servers to determine the most compatible server for the player.</li>\n<li>Matchmaking matches the player to the server with the highest score.</li>\n<li>The player joins the winning server.</li>\n</ol>\n<figcaption>Roblox default matchmaking</figcaption>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"The default Roblox matchmaking flow.\" /></p>\n<figcaption>Custom matchmaking</figcaption>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"The custom matchmaking flow.\" /></p>\n<h2>Scoring</h2>\n<p>The matchmaking scoring algorithm uses the weighted sum (WS) of signal values to assign scores to servers.</p>\n<p>For more information on scoring, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/scoring\">Server scoring</a>.</p>\n<h2>Attributes</h2>\n<p>Attributes are properties used in matchmaking scoring. An attribute can be a number, like a player's age and skill level rating, or a string, like a player's language.</p>\n<p>Attributes can be numerical or categorical:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Numerical attributes</strong> compare the difference between the joining player's attribute and the server's aggregated value, with larger differences lowering or increasing the score. For example, the closer the skill level of a player is to the average skill level of the server, the higher the numerical signal's score is. This score is then multiplied by the signal's weight.</li>\n<li><strong>Categorical attributes</strong> are based on how common the joining player's attribute is when compared to the other players in the server. For example, if a high percentage of the players inside a server have the same preferred language as the joining player, the score increases. This score is then also multiplied by the signal's weight.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For a list of all existing attributes, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/attributes-and-signals\">Existing attributes</a>. For more information about custom attributes, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/attributes-and-signals\">Custom attributes</a>.</p>\n<h2>Signals</h2>\n<p>Signals used in server scoring can either be the default ones already provided by Roblox, or custom ones you create yourself.</p>\n<p>Roblox signals use existing attributes that Roblox already has access to, like player location, age group, and latency. Custom signals, however, need to use custom attributes to access data from your data stores. In order to create a custom signal, you must first create a custom attribute.</p>\n<p>For a list of all existing Roblox signals, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/attributes-and-signals\">Existing signals</a>. For more information about custom signals, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/attributes-and-signals\">Custom signals</a>.</p>\n<h2>Custom matchmaking</h2>\n<p>When a player joins your game, matchmaking assigns a score to each running server based on the weighted sum of the server's signal values. You can customize this process by adjusting the weight of each of your signals to change how much each signal matters in the scoring of servers. Increasing the weight of the latency signal, for example, means the matchmaking algorithm prioritizes servers with lower ping for smoother gameplay.</p>\n<p>After adjusting signal weights, you can preview the impact of these new weights on matchmaking server selection by evaluating them on mock servers.</p>\n<p>For more information about how to customize matchmaking and simulate the results, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/customize-matchmaking\">Customize your matchmaking configuration</a>.</p>\n<h2>Analytics</h2>\n<p>Matchmaking analytics helps you track the success of your matchmaking configurations and find opportunities to optimize them.</p>\n<p>For more information about matchmaking analytics, see <a href=\"#/docs/matchmaking/analytics\">Analytics</a>.</p>","kid":{"simple":"Matchmaking is how Roblox decides which server a player joins when they press Play. It looks at all the servers that have room, gives each one a score, and sends the player to the server with the highest score. Scores are based on things like friends, language, age, and ping. You can change how much each thing matters so players land in the best server for your game.","analogy":"It is like a school cafeteria. When a new kid walks in, they look at every table with an open seat and pick the one with their friends, people who speak their language, or people their age.","keyPoints":["Matchmaking matches players to servers based on things like age, language, skill level, and game preferences.","Step by step: a player asks to join, matchmaking finds eligible servers, scores them, and sends the player to the top one.","Servers that are full, private, reserved, or about to shut down are skipped.","Scores come from a weighted sum: each signal's score is multiplied by its weight, then all are added up.","Attributes are raw facts like age or language. Signals turn those facts into scores from 0 to 1.","Roblox has built-in signals, and you can make custom signals using custom attributes from your data stores.","You can change signal weights and test them on pretend (mock) servers before using them for real.","Matchmaking analytics show how well your settings are working."],"terms":{"server":"One running copy of your game with its own group of players.","eligible server":"A server the player is allowed to join: not full, not private, not reserved, and not shutting down.","attribute":"A fact about a player or server. It can be a number, like age 12, or text, like language `English`.","numerical attribute":"An attribute that is a number. Scores compare the player's number to the server's average, like skill 50 vs a server average of 48.","categorical attribute":"An attribute that is a word or category, like language. Scores depend on how many players in the server share it.","signal":"A rule that turns an attribute into a score between 0 and 1. 1 means a great fit, 0 means a poor fit.","weight":"How much a signal matters. A signal with weight 3 counts three times as much as one with weight 1.","weighted sum":"Multiply each signal score by its weight, then add them up. Scores 0.5 (weight 2) and 1 (weight 1) give 0.5 x 2 + 1 x 1 = 2.","latency":"How long data takes to travel between the player and the server, also called ping. Lower is smoother.","mock server":"A pretend server you set up to test how your matchmaking settings would choose."},"watchOut":["Thinking you must set up matchmaking before your game works. Roblox's default configuration already runs for every game.","Trying to make a custom signal first. You need to create a custom attribute before you can build a custom signal from it.","Changing weights without testing. Use mock servers to preview what your new weights will do."]}}